### The harness's own check on the normalization rules is red at this head

This lane ran the harness's normalization tests directly against the committed tree:

```
node --test reference-harness/runner/test/normalize.test.mjs
```

Three tests fail. The failures are not byte mismatches — each one is a grade that comes
back `invalid` because the grader refuses the results under it:

- "the oracle's own outputs, regraded as an engine's results, pass" fails with
  `actual: [ 'results: render Missing executed-source binding', 'results: semantics
  Missing executed-source binding', 'results: scenarios Missing executed-source binding' ]`
  against `expected: []`.
- "N3 changes nothing on the committed expected bytes, which are already in sorted
  class-token order" fails with `'invalid' !== 'pass'`.
- "synthetic-v1: the masks are the measured nondeterminism" fails on the same grade.

The cause is in the metadata, and it is dated. The committed corpus
`reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1/expected/run-meta.json` records each of the
three kinds with a checkout identity of commit `28f0278139c373237fafb1cd04a35bd7a41d12e3`
and `dirty: false`, and carries **no `source_binding` key** — it never has, across the
corpus's whole history. The grader's `checkRunRecord` calls `sourceBindingProblems`
on that field, and `adapter-source-identity.mjs:257` throws "Missing executed-source
binding" when it is absent. The requirement was added on 2026-10-31 by `54ae271cc`
"Bind captured candidate grades to observed committed execution", which is an ancestor
of the integration tip; the corpus was captured on 2026-10-08 and was not recaptured.

The corpus's bytes are right. The counts the failing test expects — 1754 rendering,
2389 semantics, 16 persistence, 19 side effects, 8 read-writes — are exactly the
saved grades' numbers. What is missing is the binding, not the content.

**The triage lead has ruled this one.** Verdict 8: a real engine bug in the comparison
harness, and the corpus and the three tests are right. The witness belongs in
qualification, where the spec puts it — "Candidate source qualification also requires
`source_binding` on each run kind" and "A missing, changed, untracked, unreadable or
redirected source file leaves the capture available for diagnosis but prevents
qualification" — and the grader pushed it into the grade's problems, where the verdict
rule turns any problem into "invalid". The command line decides "NOT A GRADE" from the
verdict alone and never reads `source_qualification.qualified`, so the witness had to
be moved into problems to have any force. The fix moves it back and teaches the
command line to refuse an unqualified run.

Two things the verdict settles that this lane had recorded as open questions:

- **Recapturing the corpus is the wrong fix.** It would change every committed expected
  byte and void every comparison record already made against them, including the 1,436
  raw differences in rows 1 and 2, and it would repeat at the next rule change.
- **Changing the three tests is the wrong fix.** It would delete the only check that the
  N1 mask and the N3 class-token-order rule leave the committed expected bytes
  unchanged, which is the rule rows 1 and 2 rest on.

The fix owner is the source-custody lane, branch
`fleet/mm-255-native-source-custody-20261101`, head `87f1f1a23ecced588407519b931b63c10346eb7a`,
whose ACCEPTed source-custody work is already merged as `3701d069f`. A reviewer is
still needed.

The guard that must fail if this ever returns: a test that a capture with a valid
checkout identity, matching bytes and no witness is refused by the command line with
exit status 2 and "NOT A GRADE", and reports no pass rate. No test covers that case
today, which is why the overreach went unnoticed.

This lane has not fixed anything, and it did not call this.

